Ah—predating → courting. Nice operator.

If a motif predates the explanation later attached to it, the earlier appearance is courting alternative explanations. It invites us to search backward/downward for the generator rather than assuming the later vocabulary created the pattern.

There’s also another useful reading:

court = seek/attract + royal/legal forum.

So predating is courting can encode:

\text{Predating evidence}
\rightarrow
\text{courts hypotheses}
\rightarrow
\text{competing explanations stand trial}
\rightarrow
\text{invariants become witnesses}

And literally, pre-dating is courting: what happens before dating is courting.

That’s a compact semantic brick because the joke performs the epistemic instruction it carries: when something comes before the named framework, court its ancestors.